; Appended after the base policy (workspace-write confinement only).
;
; Process-inspection binaries the kernel cannot execute inside a sandbox.
;
; `ps` and `top` are installed setuid-root on macOS (`-rwsr-xr-x`) because they
; read the process table of every user. A sandboxed process may not transition
; to a setuid image, and that refusal happens in the kernel before the profile
; is consulted: a `(deny default)` profile AND a `(allow default)` profile both
; fail `execve` with EPERM, and no SBPL predicate exists to grant it
; (`process-exec`, `process-exec*` and `process-exec-interpreter` all fail;
; `(with setugid)` is not a sandbox variable). An unprivileged setgid copy of a
; plain binary reproduces it, so this is the setuid/setgid rule itself, not an
; entitlement these two binaries carry.
;
; `(with no-sandbox)` is the only predicate that lifts the refusal, and it is
; checked by the kernel for the exact image named, at exec time. It is granted
; ONLY here, ONLY for the two fixed, root-installed, read-only process
; observers: both exist to READ the process table and write nothing. Any other
; setuid binary — `sudo`, `crontab`, `at`, `su`, `login`, `traceroute`,
; `newgrp`, `quota` — keeps the sandbox and therefore keeps failing closed.
;
; The child is unconfined, so the write fence does not apply to it. That is an
; accepted, narrowly-scoped cost of the OS constraint; CodeLeveler's Auto
; contract states that ordinary system diagnosis is allowed, and the
; alternative is a policy that says ALLOW while the sandbox answers EPERM.
;
; A caller cannot reach this by copying a binary: the match is a `literal`
; path, and a copy is neither root-owned nor setuid, so it stays sandboxed.
(allow process-exec (with no-sandbox) (literal "/bin/ps"))
(allow process-exec (with no-sandbox) (literal "/usr/bin/top"))
